    "body": "### Problem\n\nIn the Windows nightly build v0.12.3, the Treesitter highlighting module fails to attach to buffers, even when the parser successfully builds a valid syntax tree.\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\n1. Install `nvim-treesitter` and compile the `tsx` parser (using Zig or MSVC).\n2. Open a `.jsx` file containing standard React components (e.g., `<input />`, `<button>`).\n3. Check the syntax tree via `:InspectTree` — it correctly shows `jsx_element > jsx_opening_element`. The parser works.\n4. Run `:lua print(vim.treesitter.highlighter.active[vim.api.nvim_get_current_buf()] ~= nil)` -> It returns `false` (module is not attached).\n5. Try forcing the highlighter: `:lua local p = vim.treesitter.get_parser(0, \"tsx\"); vim.treesitter.highlighter.new(p)` -> It silently fails and does not attach.\n6. `vim.treesitter.start(0, \"tsx\")` returns `nil` instead of `true`/`false`.\n\n\n### Expected behavior\n\n`highlighter.new` should successfully attach to the buffer and highlight `@tag` captures.\nThe highlighter module silently fails. Keywords are only highlighted by the LSP, while TSX tags remain unhighlighted, and `:Inspect` on tags returns \"no items found\".\n\n### Nvim version (nvim -v)\n\nNVIM v0.12.3\n\n### Vim (not Nvim) behaves the same?\n\nunknown\n\n### Operating system/version\n\nWindows 10/11\n\n### Terminal name/version\n\ncmd\n\n### $TERM environment variable\n\nstd\n\n### Installation\n\nnvim-64.msi",
